WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.330
Three Claude models get the same brief, the same tools and the same critic.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:05.698
Each builds a beach house.

00:00:05.698 --> 00:00:07.065
Which one would you keep?

00:00:09.582 --> 00:00:10.507
Hi, I'm Dormer!

00:00:10.507 --> 00:00:14.514
Today, three beach houses built from one brief, and what each one cost.

00:00:18.425 --> 00:00:19.123
Chapter one.

00:00:19.123 --> 00:00:20.518
One brief, one loop.

00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:27.469
A browser can't trace soft sunlight through a balustrade.

00:00:27.469 --> 00:00:31.516
So the light is computed once in Blender and pasted onto every

00:00:31.516 --> 00:00:32.865
surface as a texture.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:37.728
A Python script builds the whole house from nothing.

00:00:37.728 --> 00:00:39.902
No downloaded models, textures or skies.

00:00:39.902 --> 00:00:41.714
Then Cycles bakes the light.

00:00:42.134 --> 00:00:45.972
The geometry and atlases are packed into one file of about five

00:00:45.972 --> 00:00:49.490
megabytes, and headless Chrome screenshots it in this site's own viewer.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.462
Then a brand new subagent gets the brief and the pictures, never

00:00:53.462 --> 00:00:57.310
the code, and answers yes or no, with at most five ranked fixes.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.288
Fix the worst thing first, and go again, until a critic says yes.

00:01:03.675 --> 00:01:07.622
Sonnet wrote its own geometry library, so the house is about four

00:01:07.622 --> 00:01:10.253
thousand flat faces before Blender ever sees it.

00:01:10.673 --> 00:01:14.481
Cycles bakes two passes per face, colour at eight samples and light

00:01:14.481 --> 00:01:17.020
at four hundred, and the script multiplies them.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.766
The file came out over budget, so it turned the JPEG quality

00:01:20.766 --> 00:01:22.152
down instead of baking again.

00:01:22.572 --> 00:01:24.700
And on the third round, the critic said yes.

00:01:26.675 --> 00:01:27.223
Chapter two.

00:01:27.223 --> 00:01:28.593
Three ways to build it.

00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:36.764
Opus baked colour and light together, ran seven passes, and dressed the

00:01:36.764 --> 00:01:40.322
site best: a dune fence, a fire pit, a stepped boardwalk.

00:01:40.742 --> 00:01:43.651
It also repainted the siding blue grey on its own.

00:01:43.651 --> 00:01:45.396
Four critics, and not one yes.

00:01:46.883 --> 00:01:48.955
Fable never asked Cycles for colour.

00:01:48.955 --> 00:01:53.100
It baked only light, denoised it hard, and painted boards and tiles

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.481
on afterwards in Python.

00:01:54.901 --> 00:01:57.537
So the rail shadows on this balcony stay sharp.

00:01:57.537 --> 00:02:01.052
It needed five rounds, and its last four baked on a CPU.

00:02:02.883 --> 00:02:05.997
Sonnet's house is the crispest and the smallest file.

00:02:05.997 --> 00:02:09.803
Clean lines, black framed sliders, the biggest glazing of the three.

00:02:10.223 --> 00:02:13.122
But the furniture is plain boxes, and the rooms are thin.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:15.424
Chapter three.

00:02:15.424 --> 00:02:16.983
The race, and the verdicts.

00:02:21.658 --> 00:02:25.352
Here are the three loops on one clock, from launch to the finished file.

00:02:25.352 --> 00:02:26.935
Each dot is a critic's verdict.

00:02:27.355 --> 00:02:27.636
Sonnet.

00:02:27.636 --> 00:02:30.164
No, no, then yes, in about forty two minutes.

00:02:30.164 --> 00:02:31.849
The fastest yes on the page.

00:02:32.269 --> 00:02:32.564
Opus.

00:02:32.564 --> 00:02:36.399
Four critics, four noes, the last one close, at about forty eight minutes.

00:02:36.399 --> 00:02:37.874
It never got a yes.

00:02:38.294 --> 00:02:38.597
Fable.

00:02:38.597 --> 00:02:42.230
Three noes, then two yeses in a row, at about ninety three

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.349
minutes, across two days and three machines.

00:02:44.769 --> 00:02:47.775
Then three blind judges ranked matched views of all three.

00:02:47.775 --> 00:02:50.780
The cheapest yes came last with every one of them.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.162
Across thirty three view judgements, Fable took seventeen first places and Opus sixteen.

00:02:57.162 --> 00:02:58.657
Opus won every aerial.

00:02:58.657 --> 00:03:03.145
Fable won the deck, the balcony and the bathroom with every judge.

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.116
Three limits on that result.

00:03:07.683 --> 00:03:11.346
The judges are Claude models judging Claude models, one pinned to each.

00:03:11.766 --> 00:03:14.901
I placed the cameras by one rule, but the floor plans differ,

00:03:14.901 --> 00:03:16.469
so the spots are my call.

00:03:16.889 --> 00:03:18.922
And three judges looked at still frames.

00:03:18.922 --> 00:03:20.954
Nobody walked the houses for the ranking.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.278
So remember: a critic's yes means nothing is visibly broken.

00:03:25.278 --> 00:03:27.620
It does not mean the house is good.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.376
Rounds are what you pay for.

00:03:30.692 --> 00:03:32.836
Bake the light and draw it unlit.

00:03:32.836 --> 00:03:35.287
Sonnet got the fastest yes and came last.

00:03:35.287 --> 00:03:38.350
Fable won narrowly, and Opus is best from the air.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:39.269
Thanks for watching!

00:03:39.689 --> 00:03:41.790
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:42.390
I'm Dormer.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:42.690
Bye!
